    {
      "id": "93dd480f640e11d94a880fd56bfa1d3394084b2a",
      "short_id": "93dd480f",
      "created_at": "2026-08-27T22:13:10.000-04:00",
      "parent_ids": [
        "1262275a914acde907d062c5c8d30d8a4a9b970a"
      ],
      "title": "dfilter: Limit the macro expansion depth",
      "message": "dfilter: Limit the macro expansion depth\n\nThe full macro string increases in size exponentially with the depth,\nand depth 32 is too large for the current implementation strategy,\nand perhaps any strategy given that the sheer amount of memory\nnecessary to contain a fully expanded string assuming that all the\nmacros double the size of what they replace. Macros which are simply\nthe identity function would work, but why?\n",
      "author_name": "John Thacker",
      "author_email": "johnthacker@gmail.com",
      "authored_date": "2026-08-27T22:13:10.000-04:00",
      "committer_name": "John Thacker",
      "committer_email": "johnthacker@gmail.com",
      "committed_date": "2026-08-27T22:13:10.000-04:00",
      "trailers": {},
      "extended_trailers": {},
      "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/wireshark/wireshark/-/commit/93dd480f640e11d94a880fd56bfa1d3394084b2a"
    },
    {
      "id": "1262275a914acde907d062c5c8d30d8a4a9b970a",
      "short_id": "1262275a",
      "created_at": "2026-08-27T22:12:39.000-04:00",
      "parent_ids": [
        "186495f4e78976032fb534ceadacaacfb95ba1dd"
      ],
      "title": "dfilter: Don't allow a replaced macro to be replaced at greater depth",
      "message": "dfilter: Don't allow a replaced macro to be replaced at greater depth\n\nBehave as the C preprocessor does according to \"6.10.5.4 Rescanning and\nfurther replacement\":\n\n\"The resulting preprocessing token sequence is then rescanned, along with all\nsubsequent preprocessing tokens of the source file, for more macro names to\nreplace.\n\nIf the name of the macro being replaced is found during this scan of the\nreplacement list (not including the rest of the source file’s preprocessing\ntokens), it is not replaced. Furthermore, if any nested replacements\nencounter the name of the macro being replaced, it is not replaced. These\nnonreplaced macro name preprocessing tokens are no longer available for\nfurther replacement even if they are later (re)examined in contexts in which\nthat macro name preprocessing token would otherwise have been replaced.\"\n\nIn other words, a macro can be used again on the *same* nesting level\nwhen scanning the rest of the text (that's the \"not including the rest\nof the source file's preprocessing tokens), but can't be used again on\na greater nesting level.\n\nThis prevents cycles, which means that the only want to have a nesting\nlevel that reaches the maximum is to do is honestly, with 32 unique\nmacros that call one another. That's still almost surely way too large,\nsince it increases in size exponentially. The 32 nesting limit appears\nto have been chosen arbitrarily when macros were first introduced.\n\nThanks to AISLE Security for the suggestion.\n",
      "author_name": "John Thacker",
      "author_email": "johnthacker@gmail.com",
      "authored_date": "2026-08-27T22:00:11.000-04:00",
      "committer_name": "John Thacker",
      "committer_email": "johnthacker@gmail.com",
      "committed_date": "2026-08-27T22:12:39.000-04:00",
